# Container/namespace only — NOT tagged event. The rural climax: ONE decisive battle for the
# provincial capital (Chilpancingo) that, won, cracks the authority of the state and opens a
# negotiation. Modeled on the real Battle of Santa Clara. Gated on a worn army + a real force + deep water.

# =====================================================================
# EL MOMENTO — the one chance. Offered when the army is worn, the column real, the water deep.
# =====================================================================
@el_momento
title: El momento
new-page: true
face-image: img/guerrero_sierra.jpg
subtitle: The army is broken enough that a capital is within reach — but reaching for it means ceasing to be a guerrilla
tags: event
view-if: banda = "sierra" and f_phase = "marcha" and f_capital = 0 and f_desgaste >= 55 and f_hombres >= 90 and apoyo_popular >= 55 and time >= f_sc_offer_last + 3
priority: 8
frequency: 100
max-visits: 8
on-arrival: f_sc_offer_last = time

= El momento

Something has shifted, and every veteran in the column can feel it. The garrisons no longer patrol at night. The reinforcements come slower each time, and angrier, and greener. The desgaste of three years of ambushes and empty encirclements has hollowed the army in Guerrero from the inside — it still has the tanks and the helicopters, but it has stopped believing it can win, and an army that has stopped believing is an army that can be *broken.* For the first time, the thing Che dreamed and only Fidel achieved is on the table: not another ambush, but a **capital.** Chilpancingo — the seat of the state's authority in Guerrero — held by a garrison of some two thousand men — which you could now, if you concentrated every column and every reservist the villages have promised, actually take. It is not a band that storms a capital. It is an army, and you finally have one.

And there is the word that should frighten you: *concentrate.* Everything that has kept you alive — dispersal, movement, the refusal to ever be a fixed target — you would have to set aside for one throw of the dice. This is the decision Che got wrong in Bolivia and right, once, at Santa Clara. Take the capital and the whole country sees that the state does not control its own territory. Fail, concentrated, and the foco dies in a single afternoon.

- @concentrar: **Concentrar para el asalto.** Gather the scattered column into a striking force and go for Chilpancingo. The one throw. *(→ the battle)*
- @seguir_foco: **Seguir siendo guerrilla.** Not yet. Stay dispersed, keep bleeding them, let the army rot another season before you risk everything. *(decline; wear them down further; the moment will come again)*

@concentrar
go-to: santaclara.santa_clara
on-arrival: f_asalto_listo = 1

@seguir_foco
on-arrival: {! Q.f_desgaste += 4; Q.f_moral += 3; !}

You let it pass. A guerrilla's discipline is mostly the discipline of *not* fighting the battle the enemy — or your own hunger for a decision — wants you to fight before its time. The column melts back into the sierra, and for another season you are what you have always been: everywhere and nowhere, wearing the garrisons down one road, one payroll, one sleepless night at a time. The moment will come again, riper. Or it will not, and you will have been right to wait.

- @root: Continuar.

# =====================================================================
# LA BATALLA — Chilpancingo. Pick the plan. Win decisively, win at a cost, or lose the foco.
# =====================================================================
@santa_clara
title: El asalto a Chilpancingo
new-page: true
face-image: img/molotov.jpg
subtitle: One battle, for the capital and the authority of the state

= El asalto a Chilpancingo

You bring them down out of the sierra in the dark — every column, every comando, the reservists from the villages who said they would come when it was time and, this once, came. By dawn the capital is ringed by people the state swore did not exist. Inside: a garrison, a governor's palace, a radio station, and the reinforcement column already grinding up the highway from Acapulco to save them. How you take a city decides whether you take it at all.

- @sc_tren: **La columna de refuerzo primero.** Do what Che did at Santa Clara — take the relief column on the road before it arrives, strip its weapons, and leave the garrison isolated and hopeless. *(rewards materiel, intelligence, and a worn enemy — the clever throw)*
- @sc_directo: **Asalto directo.** Storm the garrison at once, before the reinforcements come, and win it with speed and numbers. *(rewards fighters and morale — the frontal throw)*
- @sc_pueblo: **Que se levante la ciudad.** Call the general strike, the barrios, the students — take the capital with the people and not only the column. *(rewards deep popular support — the insurrection throw)*

@sc_tren
go-to: santaclara.sc_resolve
on-arrival: {! var _r = (Q.f_parque*0.35)+(Q.inteligencia*0.5)+(Q.f_desgaste*0.45)+(Q.f_moral*0.2)+(Q.f_hombres*0.25)-92+(Math.random()*40-15); Q.f_sc_result = (_r>=22?"win":(_r>=0?"costly":"loss")); Q.f_sc_plan = "tren"; !}

You put the best of the column across the Acapulco road at a cut where trucks must crawl, and you spend everything you saved for this. The relief column noses into the ambush at first light —

@sc_directo
go-to: santaclara.sc_resolve
on-arrival: {! var _r = (Q.f_hombres*0.55)+(Q.f_moral*0.45)+(Q.apoyo_popular*0.15)-95+(Math.random()*40-15); Q.f_sc_result = (_r>=22?"win":(_r>=0?"costly":"loss")); Q.f_sc_plan = "directo"; !}

No cleverness, no waiting — you throw the whole force at the garrison in the grey before dawn, betting that speed and numbers and three years of rage will carry the walls before the highway delivers help —

@sc_pueblo
go-to: santaclara.sc_resolve
on-arrival: {! var _r = (Q.apoyo_popular*0.7)+(Q.f_hombres*0.3)+(Q.f_desgaste*0.35)-88+(Math.random()*40-15); Q.f_sc_result = (_r>=22?"win":(_r>=0?"costly":"loss")); Q.f_sc_plan = "pueblo"; !}

You do not take the city so much as *ask* it to rise, and it does — the market closes, the buses stop, the students pour out, the barrios put their bodies in the streets between your fighters and the tanks —

