Q.intervencion_riesgo+=6; Q.legitimidad_regimen-=3; Q.f_moral+=6; Q.f_desgaste=100;
Q.f_traicion_riesgo=1;
!}
subtitle: The oldest gamble in the Mexican countryside

You take what is offered and keep what won it. The reforms go on paper, the Party is registered, the cameras roll in Guerrero — and the guns stay oiled in the sierra, because you have read the history of this country and you know what a Mexican accord is worth to the government the morning after the cameras leave. It is the harder, wiser, lonelier road: you are betting you can hold the political gains *and* the military insurance long enough that the state cannot simply wait you out and then break its word over your grave. Sometimes it works. Zapata took a safe-conduct to a hacienda called Chinameca, once, and history remembers how that ended. You will not make his mistake. You tell yourself that.

- @root: Continuar.

@neg_seguir
new-page: true
on-arrival: {!
Q.f_negociado=3; Q.f_moral+=8; Q.leyenda=(Q.leyenda||0)+4; Q.intervencion_riesgo+=22; Q.legitimidad_regimen-=6;
Q.f_desgaste=100; Q.f_cerco=25;
!}
subtitle: The state is on its knees — and so, now, is the clock in Washington

You send the man with the soft hands back the way he came. There is no negotiating with a state you can *beat*, and Chilpancingo proved you can beat it — so you press. More of Guerrero, then Oaxaca, then the word carried on the corridos to every sierra in the south: *it can be done.* It is the boldest road and the one with the shortest fuse, because a red flag over a Mexican state capital is not a headline the giant to the north reads calmly. Somewhere in an office in Washington, a man is being shown the same photograph that thrilled you, and he is not asking whether to act. He is asking how much.

- @root: Continuar.
